            "text": "{\"primary_meaning\":\"Final divine judgment, ultimate destruction, and the place of eternal punishment for the wicked (commonly translated as hell).\",\"primary_basis\":[\"Matthew 10:28\",\"Matthew 23:33\",\"Mark 9:43-48\",\"Jeremiah 7:31-32\"],\"alternatives\":[{\"meaning\":\"A metaphor for complete annihilation or conditional immortality, where the wicked are entirely consumed and cease to exist rather than being eternally tormented.\",\"basis\":[\"Matthew 10:28\"],\"prominence\":\"minority\"},{\"meaning\":\"A prophetic symbol for the historical, national judgment and destruction of Jerusalem (often associated with AD 70), mirroring Old Testament prophecies of slaughter in the valley.\",\"basis\":[\"Jeremiah 19:6-13\",\"Matthew 23:33-38\"],\"prominence\":\"minority\"},{\"meaning\":\"A literal, perpetually burning garbage dump outside Jerusalem used as a pastoral metaphor for being discarded or destroyed as refuse.\",\"basis\":[\"Medieval Rabbinic tradition (David Kimhi) applied to New Testament texts\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"A temporary state of fiery purification or remedial punishment leading eventually to universal salvation.\",\"basis\":[\"Mark 9:49\"],\"prominence\":\"rare\"}],\"has_settled_consensus\":true,\"confidence\":\"high\",\"notes\":\"While Gehenna is near-universally recognized as the primary New Testament term for final judgment, theologians debate whether it represents eternal conscious torment, complete annihilation, or historical national judgment. Furthermore, the popular conception of Gehenna as a literal burning garbage dump is widespread in sermons but is heavily disputed by contemporary biblical historians as a medieval invention rather than a first-century reality.\"}",
